<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!-- The platform's shared database resilience: patience where a connection is opened
         (`PatientPgDriver`), one bounded retry for work that must survive a postgres cutover
         (`DbRetry`), and nothing else.

         AS THIN AS qits-arch-rules, and for the same two reasons. It carries no CDI bean, so it
         needs no quarkus-arc and no jandex index — both classes are reachable without a request
         context, which is what lets a background worker call them. And it does not depend on
         hibernate-core: the JDK's own `java.sql` exceptions are matched by type, and Hibernate's
         `JDBCConnectionException` BY FULLY-QUALIFIED NAME, so a consumer's ORM and this module's
         version stay uncoupled and a bare clone builds with no platform registry.

         The price is qits-arch-rules': a rename in Hibernate silently un-matches. The test sources
         carry a mirror of the type for that reason — the drift surfaces there first.

         Three dependencies. jboss-logging, the logging API every Quarkus application already holds
         — a retry that says nothing is a service that goes quiet for fifteen seconds with no record
         of why. And two in PROVIDED scope, pgjdbc and quarkus-narayana-jta; see each dependency's
         own note. -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>eu.wohlben.qits</groupId>
        <artifactId>qits-integrations-quarkus</artifactId>
        <version>2026.1105.105731</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>qits-db-core</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>
    <name>qits-db-core</name>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jboss.logging</groupId>
            <artifactId>jboss-logging</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <!-- PROVIDED, never compile: `PatientPgDriver` delegates to pgjdbc, and every consumer
                 already ships it inside quarkus-jdbc-postgresql. A compile-scope dependency here
                 would hand a second copy to every consumer of this module — including the ones that
                 only want `DbRetry` — and make this module's pgjdbc version a fleet-wide pin.
                 Provided is on the test classpath too, so the suite compiles and runs. -->
            <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <!-- PROVIDED, for the same bargain as pgjdbc above. `DbRetry.inNewTx` opens the
                 transaction it retries, so it must NAME `QuarkusTransaction`, which lives here.

                 Provided is what keeps that from being felt anywhere else: it is not transitive, so
                 no consumer inherits a JTA it did not ask for, and this module's Quarkus version
                 never becomes a fleet-wide pin. Every consumer of `inNewTx` already has JTA — a
                 transaction is what it is asking for — and a consumer that only wants `DbRetry.call`
                 or `PatientPgDriver` gets nothing new.

                 The alternative considered and rejected: a functional seam, `inNewTx(TxRunner, …)`
                 supplied by the consumer. It would cost nothing in weight and everything in
                 correctness — the classification below is only sound because THIS code owns the
                 transaction boundary and therefore knows a body failure was rolled back. A seam
                 hands that knowledge to the caller, which is the caller this method exists to
                 protect from getting it wrong.

                 The no-CDI-context property survives: `QuarkusTransaction` needs a running
                 application, not a request scope, so a background worker still calls it. Nothing
                 here is a CDI bean and this module still needs no jandex index. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-narayana-jta</artifactId>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-junit5</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- The proof rig runs `inNewTx` against a REAL postgres through a proxy the test
                 kills, so the test scope needs a real pool that enlists in a real transaction
                 manager. Mocks cannot produce the failures this class classifies. -->
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-agroal</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
            <artifactId>quarkus-jdbc-postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- Real postgres binaries resolved as ordinary Maven artifacts and spawned as a child
                 process, qits-eventstream's arrangement: a clone of this repo alone must test
                 green, so testcontainers and dev services are both out. -->
            <groupId>io.zonky.test</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres</artifactId>
            <version>${zonky.embedded-postgres.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <!-- Named per architecture on purpose: the binaries are separate artifacts and zonky
                 picks one at run time, so an unnamed arch fails in the middle of a suite rather
                 than during resolution. -->
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-linux-amd64</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-linux-arm64v8</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-linux-amd64-alpine</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.zonky.test.postgres</groupId>
            <artifactId>embedded-postgres-binaries-darwin-arm64v8</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
